<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The only thing this app does over the network: reach the laptop the owner paired it with, at the
      address that arrived in the pairing code. Nothing else. There is no analytics endpoint, no crash
      reporter, no update check and no server of this project's own - the far end is a machine
      belonging to the person holding the phone.

      The outer hop may be cleartext and the inner one is pinned mTLS to the bridge inside it. That
      is not a weakening: the bridge terminates no TLS of its own, so what this app opens is an HTTP
      Upgrade carrying TLS 1.3 records with a byte-pinned peer. See network_security_config.xml,
      where the decision and everything it costs are written out, and CleartextPolicyTest, which
      asserts it against the platform rather than describing it here.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      The first permission this application has ever asked a human for, and the only one it asks for
      at all - INTERNET is granted at install with nothing to refuse.

      It is here because a live viewfinder reads frames in OUR process. The route it replaces needed
      no permission precisely because the capture happened inside the system camera app, and that
      route did not work: on 2026-08-09 the owner could not pair by camera at all.

      What this costs, stated: a dialog the owner sees, can refuse, and can revoke later. So refusal
      is a supported state and not an error - the pairing screen shows the paste field instead, which
      needs no permission and is a route the payload's base64 form exists to make possible.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      required="false", and this line is load-bearing rather than tidy.

      Declaring CAMERA implies <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera"> with
      required="true", which asks app stores to hide the app from every device without a camera. This
      app is a terminal client whose other route needs no camera at all, so a phone without one must
      still be able to install it and pair by file. Stated explicitly because the default is a filter
      nobody chose and nobody would see.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.autofocus" android:required="false" />

    <!--
        No USE_BIOMETRIC. It was here to raise the system unlock prompt when the phone's own key had
        gone to sleep; the key is no longer bound to a recent unlock (2026-07-29), so there is no
        prompt to raise and the permission would be one this app does not use. Removing it narrows
        what the app asks for, which is the direction this manifest only ever moves in.
    -->

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.AgtermRemote">
        <!--
          adjustResize, stated rather than left to the system.

          The default is SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_UNSPECIFIED, which lets Android choose between resizing
          the window and panning it, per screen, by inspecting the content. That is why the terminal
          screen had TWO layouts with the same keyboard: sometimes the window shrank and sometimes it
          slid, and the choice was not ours. A layout that is right on average is the same defect as
          one that is wrong.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.AgtermRemote">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />

                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          There is no FileProvider in this manifest any more, and its absence is the point.

          It existed to hand the phone's own certificate out through the share sheet, because the Mac
          had no other way to learn it: the owner AirDropped a .pem and ran a command. Enrolment
          replaced that whole errand - the certificate now travels over the connection the pairing
          code authenticates - so the provider, its file_paths.xml and the directory they exposed are
          gone. This application now shares no file with anything.
        -->
    </application>

</manifest>
